course: dictionary
lesson: 6918
title: Thick-skinned words
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Some words are built from smaller parts, and the parts give you clues. Pachyderm comes from Greek: «pachys» means thick and «derma» means skin. Put them together and you get thick skin, which is what a pachyderm has.
explain: A pachyderm is a noun, so it names a thing. Its first meaning is a large animal with very thick skin, such as an elephant, a rhinoceros or a hippopotamus. Its second meaning is a joke: a big, heavy, clumsy person or thing can be called a pachyderm too.
explain: Read the story sentence: «He picked up the melon rind he had dropped, for he was a Tidy Pachyderm.» The word tidy is the clue. An animal with thick skin is not usually the one who tidies up, so the sentence is funny, and pachyderm must mean an animal with thick skin.
fact: A pachyderm is a noun, so it names a thing: a pachyderm, or some pachyderms.
fact: «Pachyderm» means a large animal with very thick skin, such as an elephant, rhinoceros or hippopotamus.
fact: «Pachyderm» can also be a humorous word for a big, heavy, clumsy person or thing.
fact: «Pachyderm» comes from Greek: «pachys» means thick and «derma» means skin.
fact: Scientists no longer group elephants, rhinos and hippos together, because they are not closely related. Today the word is used mainly for fun or in writing.
exercise: «He picked up the melon rind he had dropped, for he was a Tidy Pachyderm.» What does pachyderm mean here? | A kind of fruit | A brass band | *A thick-skinned animal | A tidy house
exercise: Pachyderm comes from Greek. Which Greek word means skin? | thick | *derma | pachyderm | pachys
exercise: A big, heavy, clumsy person can be called a pachyderm. What kind of word use is this? | A scientific name | A rule for spelling | *A humorous, joking use | A warning about danger
test: Scientists no longer group elephants, rhinos and hippos together. Why not? | *They are not closely related | They all live in the sea | They are all the same size | They are all extinct
test: Today the word pachyderm is used mainly for what? | Ordinary talk at home | Naming new animals | *Fun, or in writing | Weather reports
exam: Which two Greek parts make up pachyderm? | thick (skin) and tall (animal) | pachys (fast) and derma (bone) | *pachys (thick) and derma (skin) | derma (tall) and pachys (heavy)
